{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/13/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"320afe60\" lines=\"41-56\">\n<description>\nSlide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\nRule:\n- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.\n- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5d588b4d\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.\n- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.\n\nRule:\n- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths\n  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.\n- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.\n- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row\n  with background pixels.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length\ndiamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2f767503\" lines=\"66-81\">\n<description>\nErase each target-color object crossed by the ray pointed by the bar-and-control marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There is one three-pixel straight marker bar and one singleton control pixel adjacent to the bar center.\n- The remaining non-background color forms multiple 4-connected target objects.\n\nRule:\n- Cast a straight ray from the marker bar center in the direction away from the adjacent control pixel.\n- Find every 4-connected target-color object that intersects that ray.\n- Recolor all pixels of those hit target objects to the background; all other pixels stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The whole object is erased when the center ray touches any of its pixels; the full marker bar width is not swept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"fc7cae8d\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nAlign a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).\n- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.\n- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.\n\nKey insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5545f144\" lines=\"191-207\">\n<description>\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.\n- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the rightmost frame.\n- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable\n  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the\n  nearest one.\n- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.\n- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.\n\nKey insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\" lines=\"26-43\">\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a09f6c25\" lines=\"33-50\">\n<description>\nRecolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:\n  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.\n- Single red pixels are noise.\n\nRule:\n- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.\n- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects\n  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.\n- Leave isolated red pixels as background.\n\nKey insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a\nlabel for the object's reflection symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2ccd9fef\" lines=\"173-187\">\n<description>\nPredict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.\n- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.\n- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.\n\nRule\n- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.\n- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.\n- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
